Package: com.iosvcam.opencam
Name: iOS-VCAM OpenCam
Version: 0.6.83
Architecture: iphoneos-arm64e
Description: Open-source, editable virtual camera tweak. Runs in mediaserverd so it covers every capture client (stock Camera, TikTok, all apps). Pulls an RTMP stream on-device, decodes H264 via VideoToolbox, and overwrites the camera's OWN image buffer IN PLACE at BWNodeOutput -emitSampleBuffer: with a SINGLE GPU-accelerated VTPixelTransferSessionTransferImage (one pass, no per-frame pixel rotation, no CVPixelBuffer lock around the transfer), then passes the ORIGINAL sample buffer downstream - matching the closed vcamera's GPU sync discipline (render nodes stay pass-through; orientation is carried by the pipeline's existing metadata). Every landscape capture buffer is overwritten (landscape gate, no dedup, like the original); portrait buffers inherit OBS from the shared surface. The stock-Camera full-res still uses a separate P3-primaries transfer session so the saved photo's pixel values match its Display-P3 tag (0.6.65). Every client reads the same overwritten upstream IOSurface, so the app preview shows OBS. Fail-open everywhere (any error falls back to the real camera). AUDIO (as of 0.6.18): the microphone hook (VCamAudioMS.x + VCamAACDecoder) is compiled in - OBS audio is demuxed from the SAME RTMP stream (msg_type 8 -> AAC -> int16 PCM) and replaces the mic globally via a real-time-safe AudioUnitRender hook; fail-open and runtime-gated so it never affects the video path. 0.6.0: MILESTONE CLEANUP of the device-verified 0.5.9 stable - identical runtime behaviour, but the settled freeze-hunt A/B diagnostics (VCAM_VIDEO_DEDUP, VCAM_PRIVATE_DEDUP_KEY, VCAM_GPU_FENCE_FLUSH, VCAM_STAMP_DEST) and the dead opt-in photo-node path (VCAM_HOOK_PHOTO_NODES, always deadlocked the GPU) are removed, leaving only the live emit-overwrite path the original actually uses. Dark-scene saved-photo red remains at parity with the closed vcamera (rendered by the iOS16 deferredmediad Deep Fusion daemon, outside mediaserverd's reach - see VCAMERA_OPENVCAM_COMPLETE_REFERENCE.md). 0.6.1: fix the stock-Camera Photo-mode preview cycling (clear->stutter->gaussian-blur every ~3s at the ZSL/Live-Photo queue restart). Root cause (RE, engine @0x82650): the rotation session was not GPU-accelerated - only the transfer session was - so the rotated buffer feeding the GPU transfer was not GPU-synced. Photo mode is the only mode using the rotation path (portrait OBS -> landscape dst; TikTok/portrait dst skips it and always worked), so its preview updated only at the ~3s restart. Fix: set EnableGPUAcceleratedTransfer on the rotation session too, and use NSRecursiveLock for the rotate+transfer critical section (engine @0x823f8), both matching the closed vcamera. 0.6.2: the ~3s Photo-mode preview cycle is actually the portrait screen-preview buffer (1170x2532) being SKIPPED by the landscape-only gate and inheriting OBS by propagation from the shared landscape surface, which gaps at each ZSL/Live-Photo queue restart. 0.6.2 proved that overwriting the portrait DISPLAY buffer directly fences/wedges the GPU (OBS flashes -> freeze -> real camera), so the faithful landscape-only gate is kept. 0.6.3: fix the freeze instead by making the landscape overwrite land SYNCHRONOUSLY - set EnableGPUAcceleratedTransfer=false (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0) so the shared 2304x1728 IOSurface is written via CPU. The GPU-accelerated transfer was async and had not landed before the live-preview crop (1170x2532, downstream) read it, so the preview froze on the last frame and only refreshed at the ~3s ZSL re-read; a CPU transfer writes the bytes immediately so the preview crop always reads fresh pixels. 0.6.4: restore GPU-accelerated transfer (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=1), faithful to the closed vcamera which GPU-accelerates all its transfer sessions and the rotation session. The earlier GPU cycling was on a GPU-fence-degraded environment (a preceding gate-off build wedged the IOSurface fence, which persists until reboot); on a clean/re-jailbroken environment the GPU path should behave like the original. Gate stays ON (no wedge). Fall back to 0.6.3 (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0, CPU transfer) only if the GPU path still cycles on a genuinely clean env. 0.6.5: fully faithful to the closed vcamera - collapse the separate 709 still transfer session into the SINGLE 601 session used for every buffer (preview, video, and the full-res still), matching the original which creates three identical 601 sessions and uses only one for all overwrites. Now the full transfer config equals the original: GPU-accel on (transfer + rotation), landscape gate on (getLive && w>=h), NSRecursiveLock, no dedup, CCW90 rotation, Trim scaling, 709 primaries/transfer + 601 matrix, one session. 0.6.6: fix a front-camera mirror regression - GPU-accelerating the ROTATION session (added in 0.6.1) composes FlipHorizontalOrientation and the CCW90 rotation in the opposite order, so the left/right mirror rendered as a 180-degree rotation. Force the rotation session to the CPU path (decoupled from VCAM_GPU_ACCEL, which still drives the GPU TRANSFER session); the rotation GPU-accel flag never helped the preview cycle anyway. Transfer stays GPU. 0.6.7: fix the front-camera mirror properly by matching the original's flip axis. The original mirrors with kVTPixelRotationPropertyKey_FlipVerticalOrientation (its ONLY imported flip key - it does not import FlipHorizontal); combined with the CCW90 rotation that is the correct left/right selfie mirror. OpenVCam had used FlipHorizontalOrientation, which is exactly 180 degrees off (FlipVertical = Rotate180 of FlipHorizontal), so the front camera rendered as a 180-degree rotation instead of a mirror. Switched to FlipVerticalOrientation to match the original. 0.6.8: revert the 0.6.4-0.6.7 orientation experiments back to 0.5.9's proven rotation/mirror (FlipHorizontalOrientation, rotation session left at VT defaults) and restore CPU transfer (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0). GPU transfer had broken the front mirror (180 degrees) and re-introduced the preview cycle on this device. NOTE: OpenVCam's rotation is an APPROXIMATION (aspect-based CCW90 + a hand-rolled front FlipHorizontal); it is NOT the original's method, which drives CW90/CCW90+FlipVertical from a video-orientation value (ivar 0x100). A faithful rotation replica is tracked as separate future work. 0.6.9: FAITHFUL rotation replica (RE 0x83e20-0x83e7c, disasm re-verified this session). CORRECTION of the 0.6.8 note: the engine ivar that drives the rotation is CAMERA POSITION (AVCaptureDevicePosition, set by the setter @0x82878), NOT a video-orientation value - the dispatch is a two-way cmp #1 on that position. The original's rotation session is therefore driven by which physical camera is active: back camera (position <= 1) -> kVTRotation_CW90 with NO flip; front camera (position == 2) -> kVTRotation_CCW90 + kVTPixelRotationPropertyKey_FlipVerticalOrientation (the selfie mirror). OpenVCam now replicates this exactly: rotation direction and flip are driven by gSourcePosition (from the -sourcePosition hook), replacing the previous approximation (a single CCW90 for both cameras + a hand-rolled front FlipHorizontal). The aspect compare (VCamAutoOrientDegrees) is retained ONLY to decide whether a quarter-turn is needed, which is faithful to the original's emit-side raw-vs-prerotated selection (RE 0x8477c-0x847b4: same src/dst orientation uses the raw frame, differing uses the rotated one). Emit-side single-lock reused-buffer transfer and CPU transfer (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0) are unchanged from the device-stable 0.6.3. NET CHANGE vs 0.6.8: front camera now mirrors correctly (CCW90 + FlipVertical, direction unchanged, only the flip axis corrected H->V); back camera rotation direction changes CCW90 -> CW90 to match the original (the one behaviour needing on-device confirmation - if the back camera preview turns 180 degrees off, swap the back branch back to 270). Removed the now-dead VCAM_AUTO_ORIENT_DIR / VCAM_FRONT_AUTOMIRROR knobs. 0.6.10: device result for 0.6.9 - the faithful back-camera CW90 came out 180 degrees (upside down) on switching to the rear camera. Root cause: the original's per-position rotation KEYS (back CW90 / front CCW90, RE 0x83e20) are defined against the original's OWN decoded frame; OpenVCam's H264 decoder delivers the OBS frame at a different origin, so the literal CW90 inverts the back camera here - the rotation DIRECTION is pipeline-relative and not literally copyable, unlike the FLIP AXIS (FlipVertical) which is. Fix: CCW90 for BOTH cameras (the device-correct, known-good pre-0.6.9 direction), with only the FRONT adding the FlipVertical selfie mirror. Net: front camera mirrors correctly (FlipVertical, the genuine 0.6.7-0.6.9 fix) AND the back camera is upright again. The FLIP-axis correction is kept faithful; only the (pipeline-relative) rotation direction reverts to CCW90. 0.6.11: STEP-BY-STEP FAITHFUL REPLICATION of the closed vcamera's decode + ingest path (per-step RE, no guessing). Decode: output pixel format changed 420v(video-range) -> 420f(full-range), the format the original's decoder hardcodes (RE 0x86f3c); removed the non-original IOSurfaceProperties dstAttrs key and the non-original software-decoder fallback (single VTDecompressionSessionCreate like RE 0x87238); session props (ThreadCount=2, RealTime) and colour tags (709/709/601/chroma-left) already matched. Ingest rotation MOVED FROM EMIT TO INGEST, faithful to setYUVSampleBuffer:(0x82ddc)->create90ImageBuffer:(0x829e0): the decode thread now stores the raw frame AND a CCW90 pre-rotated copy under ONE engine lock in VCamFrameStore; the emit hot path (modifyImageBuffer: equiv) does NO rotation, only picks raw-vs-prerotated by orientation + one transfer, holding the SAME lock (== the original's single ivar-0x18 lock across ingest and emit; two locks caused the 0.5.7 GPU-fence deadlock). DECISIVE ROTATION CORRECTION: the camera-overwrite pre-rotation is create90ImageBuffer: = kVTRotation_CCW90 with NO flip and NO position check, for BOTH cameras (RE 0x82b48). The earlier "back CW90 / front CCW90+FlipVertical by camera position" (0x83e20) is a DIFFERENT code path (the run loop, ivars 0xc8/0xd0) that the camera overwrite never reads and was mis-attributed; that mistake is what put CW90 on the back camera in 0.6.9 (-> upside down on device). So 0.6.11 removes the front-camera FlipVertical entirely (the original has none; the front selfie mirror is the downstream capture pipeline's job) and rotates CCW90 for both cameras, pre-rotated on ingest. create90 detail replicated exactly: fresh CVPixelBuffer per frame with swapped W/H and IOSurface { IOSurfacePreallocPages:0, IOSurfacePurgeWhenNotInUse:1 } (RE 0x82a74-0x82af0). GPU-accel stays at the device-stable CPU setting for now (original uses GPU; revisited in the GPU step). 0.6.12: GPU step — flip VCAM_GPU_ACCEL default 0->1 so BOTH the transfer session and the ingest rotation session use EnableGPUAcceleratedTransfer=YES, faithful to the closed vcamera (RE 0x82530 transfer + 0x82650 rotation). Earlier GPU attempts (0.6.5/0.6.6) froze the preview, but those ran on the non-faithful emit-side double-rotation architecture on a GPU-fence-poisoned environment; now rotation is a single CCW90 ingest pass under one engine lock (the original's discipline), so the GPU path should behave like the original. If the preview freezes on a clean device, fall back to the device-stable 0.6.11 (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0, CPU). 0.6.13: STAYS GPU (reverted the wrong 0.6.12->CPU idea) and FAITHFULLY REPLICATES THE DECODE-OUTPUT CHAIN that was previously skipped (user audit caught it; iron rule: original works with GPU => we must replicate whatever it does, not switch to CPU). The closed vcamera does NOT hand the raw decoded CVPixelBuffer to the engine; its Helper -imageBufferToSampleBuffer:timeStamp: (RE 0x77c30) WRAPS it into a CMSampleBuffer (CMVideoFormatDescriptionCreateForImageBuffer + CMSampleBufferCreateForImageBuffer) with a SYNTHETIC MONOTONIC timestamp (counter +20.0/frame, PTS=CMTimeMake((int64)(counter*600),600), timescale 600, duration/DTS invalid — NOT the RTMP DTS/CTS), and CVPixelBufferLockBaseAddress's the buffer across the wrap (forcing the GPU-decoded pixels to land — a sync point), then -outputFrame: (0x77d24) hands the CMSampleBuffer to setYUVSampleBuffer:. OpenVCam now replicates all of this: VCamDecodeOutput wraps + locks + synthetic-timestamps, VCamFrameStore -ingestSampleBuffer: stores the CMSampleBuffer as raw (== ivar 0x50) and CCW90-pre-rotates its image buffer (== ivar 0x70). This closes the decode-output divergence and is a strong candidate for the GPU photo->video preview cycling (the lock sync + steady timestamps fix the decode-session async scheduling across the mode switch). 0.6.14: strict-fidelity refinement (user audit) — VCamFrameStore -ingestSampleBuffer: now stores the raw frame via CMSampleBufferCreateCopy (an independent copy preserving timing + format description + attachments), EXACTLY like the original's setYUVSampleBuffer: (0x82e24), instead of a bare CFRetain. Completes the ivar-0x50 replication (0.6.13 had already switched the stored object from a bare CVPixelBuffer to the CMSampleBuffer; 0.6.14 makes it a copy, not a retain). 0.6.15: raw-vs-prerotated SELECT made faithful (user audit) — VCamAutoOrientDegrees now does the original's plain orientation-CLASS comparison on integer w/h (RE 0x8477c-0x847b4: rotated iff src landscape+dst portrait OR src portrait+dst landscape; square->raw), replacing OpenVCam's aspect-ratio log-distance minimiser. Same result for typical 16:9/9:16 but robust to atypical/near-square/transient buffer geometries (which can appear at the photo<->video mode switch) — also a candidate for the remaining GPU cycling. 0.6.16: batch of strict-faithful-replication fixes from a systematic code audit (each RE-verified against _orig_vcamera.dylib). (1) ingestSampleBuffer releases raw->NULL before CMSampleBufferCreateCopy (original's order, 0x82ddc) so raw+rotated never mismatch. (2) removed the non-original 500ms frame-age watchdog; emit gates only on _bLive + frame!=NULL (0x84498). (3) removed the self-invented FigCaptureSourceConfiguration sourcePosition hook (original doesn't hook it; its position feeds the run-loop path which is external-UI only). (4) decode INPUT gives NO sample timing (numTimings=0/NULL, 0x87440) instead of RTMP DTS/CTS. (5) decode input NAL wrapped without copy (kCFAllocatorNull, 0x873ec). (6) output synthetic timestamp is a per-instance ivar (== Helper ivar 0x30), not a process static. (7) always rebuild the decode session per sequence header (original 0x86f78; dropped self-invented reuse/backoff — RISK: re-created per GOP if the encoder re-sends SPS/PPS, watch err1100). (8) RTMP disconnect sets _bLive=NO and KEEPS the last frame (original clearCache 0x837e0 never clears 0x50/0x70; setLive: from the accept callback), no longer clears the frame or invalidates the decoder. (9) rotation session created + configured at engine init (0x82650), not lazily on the first frame. (10) transfer session likewise created + configured at engine init (0x82494), moved into VCamFrameStore (== ivar 0x88) — both engine sessions now eager. Still GPU (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=1). 0.6.17: two more strict-faithful-replication audit fixes. (1) ZERO-COPY decode input: the RTMP payload was copied once (dataWithBytes NSData) before the no-copy block-buffer wrap, so the end-to-end memory chain wasn't identical. The original's -decode:size: (0x87390) takes a raw (void*, int) and wraps it no-copy (kCFAllocatorNull) straight from librtmp's reassembled contiguous m_body; decodeAccessUnit now takes (const void*, size_t) and wraps `body` directly (safe: the RTMP reader calls it synchronously and the VT decode is synchronous, so `body` outlives the decode). No behavioural change (pure memory management). (2) removed the dead mirror/rotation config knobs: VCamConfig read plist mirror/rotation but nothing on the overwrite path consumed them — leftovers from the pre-0.6.11 approximation era (same family as the removed VCAM_AUTO_ORIENT_DIR/VCAM_FRONT_AUTOMIRROR). The faithful overwrite rotates a fixed CCW90 (0x82b48) and never flips (front mirror is downstream), so the original has no such knob to drive. 0.6.18: UNIFIED AUDIO - OBS audio and video now come from ONE RTMP stream in ONE tweak. The RTMP reader already received audio tags (msg_type 8) but dropped them; now VCamRTMPSource demuxes the FLV AAC track, VCamAACDecoder decodes it (AudioConverter, configured from the stream's AudioSpecificConfig) to interleaved int16 PCM, and pushes it into the mediaserverd microphone-replacement ring that VCamAudioMS.x's AudioUnitRender hook consumes - so every capture app (stock Camera, TikTok) hears OBS audio. The hard real-time-safe consumer (lock-free SPSC ring + jitter buffer + render-thread memcpy/fade) is unchanged from the 0.8.0 media-active work; only the PRODUCER moved from the standalone :1936 PCM bridge to the shared RTMP stream (gAudioSourceRTMP=1; the legacy socket producer stays dormant so exactly one producer feeds the ring). No extra PC-side daemon or second tunnel. The AAC decode runs on the RTMP reader thread (off the render thread), audio is fail-open and runtime-gated (default on; disable flag / Enabled=false pref), and NOTHING in the known-good 0.6.17 video path changed - so audio can never wedge video. Original parity note: the closed vcamera does NOT replace the mic in mediaserverd (its audio is its own GPUImage+librtmp streaming), so this is OpenVCam's own faithful-in-spirit design, not a replication. 0.6.39: AUDIO CONSUMER REWRITTEN to the simple BROADCAST model proven in the safe AudioBridge tweak. The single-latch/steal/leak-guard/priming/dynamic-PTS-water consumer (0.6.18-0.6.38) fought TikTok's multi-unit VoiceProcessingIO: the latch landed on the wrong unit (silent recording) and the steal churn crash-looped mediaserverd. Now ANY mic-input (bus 1) render pops directly from ONE shared PCM FIFO and fills its buffer with OBS audio - no latched unit, no stealing, no priming water, no dynamic sync target (video is real-time after the 0.6.35 fps fix, so audio just plays through). Whichever unit actually renders the mic gets the audio; naturally multi-app since one app records at a time. The FIFO is a fixed int16 ring guarded by one os_unfair_lock (held only for the ring memcpy; priority-donating, strictly correct for concurrent renders, no per-render alloc). The producer is unchanged (RTMP msg_type 8 -> VCamAACDecoder -> IVCAMMediaActivePushPCM at the stream's native rate/channels); the consumer requires the render rate to match the stream (no RT resampling), channel-maps mono<->stereo at pop, handles int16/float32 interleaved/non-interleaved. Fail-open everywhere; the ONE mic-leak guard mutes (not real mic) only during a momentary underrun WHILE OBS audio is actively flowing - a video-only stream or the pre-first-frame startup falls open to the real mic. The render unit's format is NOT probed per render (AudioUnitGetProperty on every mic render across mediaserverd's many units overloads the HAL budget -> crash-loop, the 0.6.36 lesson); it is probed at most ~5x/s, packed into one atomic word, and reused - the one piece the in-process safe tweak didn't need. Removed the dormant :1936 socket producer, the resampler, and all latch/steal/priming state (1124 -> ~500 lines). 0.6.40: fix the 0.6.39 regression that FROZE stock-Camera recording (device syslog: continuous ClientHALIODurationExceededBudget HAL overload ~22x/s, recording could not get audio -> timer stuck -> aborted -> no file; video path healthy throughout). Three root causes, all fixed: (1) 0.6.39 put an os_unfair_lock on the CoreAudio RENDER thread — a known HAL-overload source (the proven 0.5.x-0.6.35 ring was lock-free). The FIFO is now LOCK-FREE: single-writer producer + CAS-claim consumers, no lock the render thread ever touches. (2) OBS audio was 44100 Hz but the mic units are 48000 Hz, so the exact-rate consumer never injected (replaced=0) and the producer flooded the ring (overflow millions). The producer now RESAMPLES to the render unit's cached rate, so the consumer always matches and drains. (3) audio was decoded+pushed even during camera preview (no mic capturing), loading mediaserverd for nothing; audio work is now IDLE-GATED — the AAC decoder skips decoding (IVCAMAudioWantsDecode) and the producer parks until a mic-input render has occurred recently. Broadcast model unchanged (any bus-1 render pops the shared FIFO). Telemetry adds wantsDecode. 0.6.41: fix the ACTUAL cause of the stock-Camera recording freeze (device watchdog bug_type 288: "Timed out waiting for capture graph source nodes start to complete"). The HAL overload chased in 0.6.39/40 was a RED HERRING — the known-good 0.6.35 logs 2183 overloads and records fine. The real regression: the 0.6.39 rewrite put a 32 KiB stack scratch (int16 src[8192*2]) inside the render function; the capture-graph audio source-node-start render thread has a small stack, so that array overflowed/stalled it, the source node never finished starting, mediaserverd timed out and reset (render stuck at 1, ~15 s restart loop). 0.6.35 used a static scratch (tiny stack) and never hit this. Fix: the consumer now reads the claimed span DIRECTLY from the ring with inline channel-mapping (IVCAMRingMapSample) — no scratch buffer at all, so the render stack stays tiny. All other 0.6.40 fixes (lock-free ring, resample-to-unit, idle-gate) are retained. 0.6.42: PROCESS-BRANCHED AUDIO (one package, two mechanisms). The 0.6.39-0.6.41 broadcast rewrite in mediaserverd kept breaking the stock Camera's capture-graph source-node START (bug_type 288), while the single-latch design (0.6.35) works there. So audio now branches by process: mediaserverd + stock Camera -> VCamAudioMS.x restored to the proven 0.6.35 single-latch; other capture apps (TikTok) -> new VCamAudioApp.xm, an in-process broadcast AudioUnitRender hook (the device-proven safe-AudioBridge model, which works in TikTok's own VoiceProcessingIO where the capture-graph-start issue does not exist). The plist now loads the dylib into mediaserverd AND TikTok; each %ctor self-gates (Tweak.xm video + VCamAudioMS audio: mediaserverd only; VCamAudioApp: non-mediaserverd, non-camera). OBS audio for the in-process hook comes from the SAME RTMP stream (VCamRTMPSource audio-only mode, no video decode/frame-store) routed via a per-process gVCamPCMSink and resampled to 48 kHz — no :1936 PC bridge needed. Fail-open everywhere. 0.6.50: FLOATING CONTROL PANEL. A draggable button injected into SpringBoard (floats over every app) opens a card with the RTMP pull URL (default rtmp://127.10.10.10:1935/live/srs) and 替换视频 / 替换音频 switches, all hot (no respring). Cross-sandbox hot control: Save writes /var/mobile/Media/vc.plist (mediaserverd reads it on its 1.5s poll for the URL + toggles) AND publishes the toggles on a Darwin-notification bus (com.iosvcam.opencam.settings, 64-bit state) that reaches mediaserverd instantly and TikTok's in-process mic path — the only channel that crosses TikTok's third-party sandbox (which cannot read the config file at all). New replaceVideo/replaceAudio config keys gate the video overwrite (Tweak.xm) and both mic paths (mediaserverd emit + the TikTok in-process hook). The panel is gated to SpringBoard, built only after a foreground scene exists, swizzles nothing, is wrapped in @try/@catch and fails open (a failure costs at most a respring); create /var/mobile/Media/vcam_nopanel to disable just the panel without uninstalling. TikTok always pulls the default URL (its sandbox can't receive the string); the URL field takes effect for mediaserverd (all video + the stock Camera). 0.6.51: DIAGNOSTIC build. Device-proven that RootHide runs TikTok tweak-free, so the in-process mic hook never loads and TikTok records the real mic (video still works — it is mediaserverd-side). Adds VCamAudioProbe.x, a LOG-ONLY mediaserverd probe (pass-through hooks on AudioUnitRender/AudioUnitProcess/AudioConverterFillComplexBuffer/AudioConverterConvertComplexBuffer, off-thread logging, no buffer edits) to LOCATE where TikTok's VoiceProcessingIO/HAL mic PCM flows in mediaserverd — so OBS audio can be injected there system-side like video (surviving RootHide), instead of guessing. Silence the probe with /var/mobile/Media/vcam_noprobe. To be removed once the mediaserverd mic-replacement point is wired. 0.6.52: DEVICE-PROVEN that TikTok's mic is processed in mediaserverd via AudioUnitProcess (active only while recording, stops when the mic is released — the camera preview keeps running). Adds an AudioComponentInstanceNew hook to the probe so each audio unit's TYPE (VoiceProcessingIO / effect / mixer) is logged at creation and matched to the AUProcess unit pointers — pinpointing the mic-input unit to overwrite (vs the echo-cancel reference/output) before wiring the real replacement. 0.6.53: DEVICE-PROVEN that TikTok's mic runs through VoiceProcessingIO's internal Apple aufx effect chain inside mediaserverd (mono float32 units firf/ldnm/tmst/cfrk/mstk/... driven by AudioUnitProcess; the stereo units are the silent echo-cancel reference). Adds a gated TONE TEST: when /var/mobile/Media/vcam_mictone exists, the AudioUnitProcess hook overwrites each mono float32 buffer with a 440 Hz sine synced to the audio clock — to confirm this point reaches TikTok's recorded mic and is crash-safe before the real OBS pipeline is wired. Default OFF (pure log-only probe); toggles live via the flag, no reboot. 0.6.54: DEVICE-CONFIRMED the tone reaches TikTok's recorded mic (beep heard) — so overwriting the mono AudioUnitProcess buffers is the correct, crash-safe, RootHide-surviving injection point. Wires the REAL OBS audio there: a mono int16 @48kHz FIFO is fed from the shared AAC decoder (wrapping gVCamPCMSink, resample+downmix on the decode thread) and the AUProcess hook pops one slice per audio period and writes it (as float) into every mono mic-effect buffer. Jitter-buffered (prime ~50ms, re-prime on underrun -> real mic). Gated behind /var/mobile/Media/vcam_micinject for this first OBS test; once confirmed it becomes default (driven by 替换音频) and the probe/tone/flags are removed. 0.6.55: fix the 0.6.54 crackle/stutter (device telemetry showed the FIFO chronically underrunning, fifo -> ~68 samples). Root cause: the mono VPIO effect units are INDEPENDENT streams (different periods/sizes 1024/256/84), so the per-period POP actually drained the FIFO once per UNIT -> ~7x over-consumption -> underrun. Replaced the draining FIFO with a lock-free ring read through a drift-corrected cursor that advances at REAL TIME (mach clock), not per call: total consumption is exactly 48 kHz no matter the unit count, a ~100 ms jitter window absorbs SRS bursts, and a resync snaps back on underrun/over-drift. Continuous like the tone. 0.6.56: polish the 0.6.55 audio — (1) the read cursor now CARRIES its fractional sample across calls (gFrac) instead of truncating ~<1 sample per call; that slow downward drift used to accumulate until the over-drift resync snapped and clicked, which was the residual "轻微滋啦" crackle. (2) the injection stays scoped to the exact mic buffer only (mNumberBuffers==1, mono, exactly n*float — excludes the stereo echo-cancel reference and the bufs=2 output/reference buffers, whose clobber broke the volume keys and added noise). (3) the A/V target latency is now LIVE-TUNABLE via /var/mobile/Media/vcam_miclat (a plain ms number, clamped 10-1000, default 100) so lip-sync can be dialled in on-device without a rebuild: lower it if audio lags the lips, raise it if audio leads. 0.6.57: replace the fixed latency with true DYNAMIC A/V (PTS) sync — the fix for "口型对不上". A fixed offset can't hold: network jitter and long-session pipeline drift shift the real audio-vs-video gap, so any single vcam_miclat number eventually mismatches. Now the mic ring locks the audio it injects onto the video shown RIGHT NOW: the H264 decoder already publishes each displayed frame's RTMP PTS (IVCAMSetVideoPTS, previously a stub — now stores pts+mach time), the AAC push tags the ring with each audio frame's RTMP PTS (one rolling anchor; audio PTS is linear in sample position), and the reader targets the audio sample whose PTS equals the video's current stream-time (its PTS extrapolated to now). A glitch-free mach-clock cursor does the playback; a slow, capped pull (±2 samples/call outside a ~2.5 ms deadband, hard-snap past 250 ms) corrects drift/offset inaudibly while rejecting per-frame decode jitter; the target is clamped to a [30 ms,400 ms] latency envelope for underrun headroom. Self-correcting, immune to phone-heat (mach + audio-hardware clocks are fixed-frequency) and network wobble — no manual number. Falls back to the fixed vcam_miclat latency before the first PTS or when /var/mobile/Media/vcam_micfixed exists (no-rebuild escape hatch). Health log now prints dyn=/averr= so the residual A/V error is visible. 0.6.58: fix the "嗡嗡" hum — the recorded OBS audio was garbled. Device telemetry (0.6.57 dyn=1, obs climbing, buf/averr swinging ±80 ms) revealed the real cause: TikTok's mic runs through ~7 mono float32 effect units that ALL call AudioUnitProcess every render, and 0.6.55-0.6.57 fed them from ONE shared read cursor — the jittering firing order scrambled whichever unit TikTok records into a hum (the 440 Hz tone stayed clean only because it indexed each buffer by that unit's OWN mSampleTime). Fix: index OBS the same way — obsPos = base + mSampleTime with a SEPARATE base per unit (small fixed table), so every unit reads a CONTIGUOUS OBS stream and the recorded one is glitch-free no matter how many units render or in what order. Per-unit re-centre snaps the base back inside the ring on clock drift / gaps. Latency is a fixed ~gLatMs behind the head (default 100 ms), tunable via vcam_miclat now that the audio is actually clean; the shared-cursor mach-clock + PTS-slew machinery (0.6.55-0.6.57) is removed. Health log prints lat=/units=. NOTE also discovered this session: on this RootHide device the real Media dir mediaserverd sandbox-resolves is /rootfs/private/var/mobile/Media (the jailbreak SSH shell's /var/mobile/Media does not exist) — flags must live there. 0.6.59: BREAKTHROUGH — 0.6.58's recording was actually CORRECT all along (the imported-to-PC file has proper OBS audio + sync). The whole "hum / no sound / lip-sync off" saga was a MEASUREMENT CONFOUND one level deeper: the inject overwrote EVERY mono float32 AudioUnitProcess buffer, and PLAYBACK output (Photos/music) uses that same format — so playing the clip on the phone re-injected live OBS/tone OVER the playback regardless of the file. Fix: gate the mic injection on IVCAMCameraActive() — a heartbeat the capture-graph emit hook (Tweak.xm VCamEmit) stamps per frame; the inject fires ONLY while a real capture runs (recording/preview), never during playback. Phone-side Photos playback is now clean AND the recorded file keeps OBS audio. Core RootHide TikTok mic replacement WORKING. (Lesson: verify the FILE off-device — import to PC — never trust on-device playback while injection is live.) 0.6.60: two TikTok-audio fixes on the mediaserverd AUProcess mic path (the RootHide-surviving injection). (1) CRACKLE ("滋滋啦啦"): the OBS->48kHz resampler in VCamMicPush reset its phase to 0 every decode block AND stopped at frames-1, so it DROPPED the last input sample of every block and restarted at a fresh sub-sample phase — a periodic discontinuity (≈ every block/48k) that TikTok recorded as a crackle (the stock Camera was clean because it uses the separate exact-format no-resample IVCAMAudioPopForEmit FIFO). Fixed: the resampler now CARRIES its fractional phase across calls and uses the previous block's last sample as the cross-block interpolation anchor, so the 48 kHz output is one continuous stream. (2) 替换音频 SWITCH now drives TikTok's mic: the inject was gated only on the /var/mobile/Media/vcam_micinject flag file (a leftover from the first OBS test) and never read the UI toggle, so the floating panel's 替换音频 switch had no effect on TikTok (and a clean install with no SSH flag gave TikTok no OBS audio at all). Now a background poller mirrors [VCamConfig shared].replaceAudio (the 替换音频 switch, default ON) into the inject gate — the legacy flag file is OR'd in as a no-UI escape hatch — so the switch controls TikTok's mic exactly like it controls the video, and OBS audio is on by default. The RT AUProcess hook still reads one atomic (no objc/config-lock on the audio thread). 0.6.61: fix cross-session TikTok crackle (device seq: 1st TikTok clean -> stock Camera -> 2nd TikTok crackles). The per-unit OBS->mic mapping table (obsPos = base + mSampleTime, one base per VPIO effect unit, added 0.6.58) was never cleared between capture sessions. CoreAudio DESTROYS those effect units when recording stops and creates FRESH ones (new pointers) next session, so across sessions dead pointers accumulated and filled the 16-slot table; once full, every new unit hit the `count<MAX ? count++ : 0` fallback and evicted to slot 0 — a whole new session's ~7 units COLLAPSED onto ONE shared base, i.e. the exact shared-cursor scramble the per-unit table exists to prevent (the pre-0.6.58 hum/crackle). The intervening stock-Camera recording is what filled the table (its mono AUProcess units also register), so the 1st TikTok was clean but the 2nd crackled. Fix: detect a new session by the multi-second gap in VCamMicRead calls (recording stops -> the mic units stop rendering; a live cadence is tens of ms) and FLUSH the whole table (>400 ms gap) so every session rebuilds a clean, collision-free set of per-unit bases with no dead pointers. Pure consumer-side state reset; the ring, resampler, gate and latency are unchanged. 0.6.62: DYNAMIC PTS A/V SYNC restored on top of the per-unit architecture — lip-sync now self-corrects over long / hot / jittery sessions, WITHOUT the 0.6.57 hum. Background: 0.6.55-0.6.57's dynamic PTS sync was drift-immune but rode a SINGLE shared read cursor that TikTok's ~7 mono VPIO effect units scrambled into a hum; 0.6.58 killed the hum with a per-unit mSampleTime base (obsPos = base + mSampleTime, one base per unit) but reverted to a FIXED gLatMs latency, so lip-sync could slowly drift as network jitter / pipeline (phone-heat) drift shifts the real A/V gap and no single vcam_miclat number holds. 0.6.62 combines both: KEEP the per-unit base (the anti-hum invariant, untouched) but make each base slowly TRACK a PTS-derived target instead of sitting a fixed distance behind the write head. The H264 decoder already publishes each displayed frame's RTMP PTS (IVCAMSetVideoPTS -> IVCAMVideoPtsMs/Host); the producer now tags the mic ring with a rolling audio-PTS anchor (VCamMicPush forwards the AAC frame's RTMP PTS: sample gAudioAnchorPos has PTS gAudioAnchorPtsMs, linear at 48 samples/ms); each render maps the video PTS shown now (extrapolated to now by the mach clock) to the ring position whose OBS-audio PTS matches it (targetPos) and nudges THIS unit's base toward it by at most ±2 samples/call outside a ~5 ms deadband (hard-snap past 250 ms). Because the base moves <=2 samples/call and playback is still base + mSampleTime, per-unit contiguity is never broken, so the audio stays hum-free while the offset self-corrects to lip-sync (audio and video ride the SAME RTMP timeline, so aligning their PTS == correct sync; immune to phone-heat + network wobble since the mach and audio-hardware clocks are fixed-frequency, no manual number). The target is always clamped into the [30 ms,400 ms] latency envelope, so a stale/absent PTS can never underrun. Falls back to the fixed gLatMs latency before the first PTS or when /var/mobile/Media/vcam_micfixed exists (no-rebuild escape hatch; vcam_miclat still sets that fallback ms). Health log adds dyn=/averr= (dynamic active + residual A/V error ms) to watch the sync on-device. Producer+consumer sync change only; the ring, resampler, gate, session flush and the whole video path are otherwise unchanged. 0.6.63: DEAD-CODE CLEANUP — remove the superseded in-process audio path so nobody walks it again. The 0.6.42 process-branched design routed non-mediaserverd capture apps (TikTok) to an in-process AudioUnitRender broadcast hook (VCamAudioApp.xm), loaded by the plist filter's com.zhiliaoapp.musically/TikTok entry. RootHide app-hiding runs TikTok TWEAK-FREE, so that dylib never loads there and the path is inert on-device; and it is redundant everywhere else because the real, device-proven TikTok mic replacement is the mediaserverd-side AudioUnitProcess VPIO hook (VCamAudioProbe.x) — which is app-agnostic and covers every app whose mic mediaserverd processes (TikTok, Shopee, Mercado, ...) with no per-app plist entry, exactly like the video overwrite. Removed: VCamAudioApp.xm (source + Makefile FILES) and the TikTok bundle/executable from OpenVCam.plist (filter is now mediaserverd + SpringBoard only). VCamControlChannel stays (the floating panel publishes toggles on it and mediaserverd's VCamConfig reads them for instant, no-poll updates). Also refreshed the stale Makefile header that still called VCamAudioProbe.x a 0.6.51 log-only diagnostic — it has been the real PTS-aligned mic replacement since 0.6.54/0.6.62. No functional change to the shipping RootHide behaviour: video (mediaserverd) and audio (mediaserverd AUProcess) are byte-for-byte the same path; only the never-executed in-process branch is gone. 0.6.64: fix the SAVED-PHOTO RED CAST (all lighting, immediate — the still thumbnail and opened photo are red while preview/record are correct). This is DISTINCT from the archived dark-scene deferred red (§5, dark-only, appears on swipe-back). Root cause: since 0.6.5 collapsed the separate still transfer session into the single 601/709 session used for every buffer, that session stamps ITU-R 709 colour tags onto the full-res still destination buffer; the still buffer is wide-gamut (Display P3), so the HEIC encoder re-colour-manages the 709-tagged pixels into P3 and pushes red (memory openvcam-photo-red-dest-stamp-p3). Preview (1728) / record (720) buffers are not re-colour-managed the same way, which is why only the photo reddened. Fix (VCAM_PHOTO_COLOR, default 1): scoped ONLY to the full-res still buffer (short side >= VCAM_STILL_MIN_DIM, default 2200), snapshot the still buffer's OWN native colour tags before the VTPixelTransfer and restore them after, overwriting the 709 the session stamps — so the saved photo is colour-managed with its native (P3) tags like the real camera. Mode 2 strips the tags instead (encoder infers from format) as a fallback. The video/preview colour path is byte-for-byte unchanged (the branch is gated by still-size, never entered by preview/record). Fail-open: any anomaly leaves the already-overwritten pixels and the photo still saves. Corrected the stale package note that claimed a separate 709 still session still existed (removed in 0.6.5). 0.6.65: the 0.6.64 tag snapshot/restore did NOTHING on-device (still red) — DEVICE-DIAGNOSED the real mechanism by pulling the saved HEIC and parsing its nclx colour box: it is tagged primaries=12 (Display P3) / transfer=1 (709) / matrix=6 (601), the NORMAL iPhone-photo tag, stamped INDEPENDENTLY by deferredmediad (it does NOT inherit the mediaserverd buffer tag — which is why restoring the buffer tag had no effect, same cross-process wall as §5). The red is in the pixel VALUES: the single 709-primaries transfer session writes 709 pixel values into the still buffer, and iOS then interprets those 709 values as the wider P3 gamut the tag declares -> every colour over-saturates toward the gamut edge, reddest first (P3 expands red most), and the reduced colour entropy also shrinks the HEIC to ~1/3 size (an independent symptom the user spotted). FIX (values, not tags): transfer the full-res still through a SEPARATE VTPixelTransferSession whose DestinationColorPrimaries = P3_D65 (VCamFrameStore -stillTransferSession, created eagerly alongside the main 709 session), so VideoToolbox gamut-maps the OBS frame 709->P3 — a 709 red becomes the P3 value for the SAME absolute colour, which then matches the P3 tag and renders correctly. VCAM_PHOTO_COLOR reworked: 0 = still uses the 709 main session (pre-fix red, for A/B), 1 (default) = still uses the P3 session. Still scoped to short side >= VCAM_STILL_MIN_DIM (2200); preview/record keep the untouched 709 main session, byte-for-byte unchanged. Fail-open: if the P3 session failed to create, the still falls back to the main session (== 0.6.64 behaviour). The 0.6.64 tag snapshot/restore code is removed. 0.6.66: DIAGNOSTIC build - the 0.6.65 P3 transfer session did NOT fix the red on device (photos pulled after install were still red), so before a third fix attempt this build proves the mechanism. Two on-device facts already point the way: (a) deferredmediad IGNORES the mediaserverd buffer colour tag (the 709 main session already stamps the still 709 yet the saved HEIC is P3-red), and (b) 0.6.64 (709 dest) and 0.6.65 (P3 dest) produced the SAME red, implying VTPixelTransferSession's DestinationColorPrimaries does NOT gamut-convert the pixel VALUES (only maybe the output tag, which is ignored). This build adds still-only, read-only, log-once diagnostics in VCamOverwriteInPlace: it dumps the still buffer's true pixel format + native/after-transfer colour attributes and the OBS src's, and runs a self-contained probe that transfers the same OBS frame into two scratch buffers via a fresh 709-dest and a fresh P3-dest VTPixelTransferSession and logs both centre samples - if identical, VT does not gamut-convert (confirming the fix must remap VALUES via vImage/ColorSync, not the dest tag). Default runtime behaviour is UNCHANGED (still uses the P3 session); the video/preview path is untouched (all new code gated by isStill). The precise value-remapping fix follows in 0.6.67 once the still format is known from these logs. 0.6.67: THE FIX. Device diagnostics confirmed it: the still buffer is natively Display-P3 (prim=P3_D65) and 10-bit LOSSLESS (fmt &xf0), the OBS src is plain 420f 709, and the 709-vs-P3 probe wrote near-identical pixel values - i.e. VTPixelTransferSession's DestinationColorPrimaries only RE-TAGS, it does not gamut-convert, so our transfer left 709-primaries VALUES under the P3 tag deferredmediad stamps -> over-saturation toward the gamut edge, reddest first = the red cast (preview/record read the same values as 709, so they were always correct). VCamStillGamut709toP3 now performs the real value remap for the full-res still ONLY: (1) VT decodes the OBS 420f 709 frame into a plain BGRA scratch (the still is lossless so vImage cannot touch it directly); (2) vImage colour-managed 709->DisplayP3 on that BGRA (Accelerate/CPU - deliberately NOT a CIContext, to avoid any GPU-fence risk in mediaserverd); (3) VT scales the P3-valued BGRA into the still buffer, which now holds true P3 values matching its P3 tag. Runtime mode is hot-selectable via /var/mobile/Media/vcam_photocolor (0=709 baseline red, 1=P3 tag only = 0.6.65, 2=gamut-map fix) so it A/Bs with no rebuild; compiled default stays 1 (unchanged) until the fix is device-confirmed, then it becomes 2. Fail-open at every step (any failure keeps the plain transfer = current behaviour, never a black/frozen frame); the video/preview/record path is byte-for-byte untouched (the whole path is gated by isStill = short side >= VCAM_STILL_MIN_DIM, and mode 2 only remaps the still's own values). Links memory openvcam-065-photo-red-p3-gamut-fix (the value-vs-tag correction) and openvcam-photo-red-dest-stamp-p3. 0.6.68: fix the hot mode flag path. 0.6.67 read /var/mobile/Media/vcam_photocolor, but device logs prove mediaserverd's sandbox CANNOT read /var/mobile/Media (VCamConfig's vc.plist is actually read from /var/tmp/vc.plist - the /var/mobile/Media candidates fail first), so the flag was never seen and every still fell back to the compiled default (mode 1 = still red). VCamPhotoColorMode now reads /var/tmp/vcam_photocolor (the proven mediaserverd-readable dir, where the log and vc.plist live), with /var/mobile/Media kept as a second try for app contexts. Set /var/tmp/vcam_photocolor to 2 to A/B the gamut fix. No other change; compiled default still 1. 0.6.69: fix the mode-2 CRASH (device: with /var/tmp/vcam_photocolor=2 every stock-Camera shutter press crashed mediaserverd repeatedly and the photo NEVER SAVED). Root cause was NOT the gamut fix but the 0.6.66 DIAGNOSTIC: VCamCentreSample CPU-reads the still buffer's centre pixel, but the full-res still is &xf0 = 10-bit PACKED LOSSLESS (AGX-compressed), whose locked base address is NOT linearly addressable — the read indexed past the mapped region and SIGSEGV'd mediaserverd. It fires in the after-xfer diag (photo-diag: still DST native logs, the very next after-xfer line never does), which runs BEFORE VCamStillGamut709toP3, so the actual fix never even executed. Fix: VCamCentreSample now gates its raw pixel fetch to a CPU-linear allow-list (420f/420v/8-bit BiPlanar + 32BGRA) and returns "(fmt-skip)" for lossless/10-bit/anything else; attachment reads (VCamDescribeBuffer) were always safe and are unchanged. With the diagnostic no longer crashing, mode 2's value remap finally runs. Compiled default still 1; test with /var/tmp/vcam_photocolor=2. 0.6.70: TUNABLE desaturation on top of mode 2. Device A/B (same scene, fix OFF vs ON) proved mode 2 is real — it cut the still's midtone red by ~1/3 (R-G +141->+95) — but a same-scene VIDEO reference (the correct 709 colour, pulled and measured: midtone R-G=+41) showed a large residual: deferredmediad RE-SATURATES the still it renders, so a straight colour-managed 709->P3 map still leaves the midtones over-red. VCamStillGamut709toP3 now applies an extra saturation factor AFTER the 709->P3 vImage step (convex blend of each pixel toward its Rec709 luma) to pre-cancel that boost, hot-tunable via /var/tmp/vcam_photosat (0..100 percent, 100=off/unchanged) so it dials in on-device against the video target with NO rebuilds. Still-only and fail-open (all inside VCamStillGamut709toP3, gated by isStill + mode 2); preview/record byte-for-byte untouched (device-proven: a 0.6.63-vs-this-build same-scene video pair measured IDENTICAL colour). photo-fix log now prints sat=. Compiled default still mode 1 / sat 100 (no-op) until the on-device tuning settles, then the winning mode+sat become the compiled defaults. 0.6.71: RED-ONLY compression replaces 0.6.70's global desaturation. Device tuning showed global desaturation (sat=50) DID match the video's midtone red and kill the cast, but it pulled EVERY colour toward luma so the photo looked washed-out / over-exposed (user: colours diluted). Fix: compress ONLY the red excess over green — where R>G, pull R toward G by (1-rk); G, B and all cool/neutral pixels are left untouched, so the picture keeps full vividness while the warm/red cast comes down. Hot-tunable via /var/tmp/vcam_photored (0..100 red-keep percent, 100=off), replacing /var/tmp/vcam_photosat; dials in on-device against the same-scene video target with no rebuilds. photo-fix log now prints redkeep=. Still-only, fail-open, preview/record byte-for-byte untouched. Compiled default still mode 1 / redkeep 100 until tuning settles. 0.6.72: BAKE THE PHOTO FIX AS THE COMPILED DEFAULT so it survives reboot and no longer depends on the fragile /var/tmp flag files (the system cleans them under load, which made every tuning shot need the flags re-set). Shipping default is now VCAM_PHOTO_COLOR=2 (the 0.6.67 exact 709->P3 value remap for the full-res still) + VCAM_PHOTO_REDKEEP=60 (the 0.6.71 red-only compression: keep 60% of each pixel's red-EXCESS-over-green, i.e. cut the residual warm cast deferredmediad re-adds by 40%, touching no green/blue/neutral/cool pixel so nothing washes out). The /var/tmp/vcam_photocolor and /var/tmp/vcam_photored flags remain as no-rebuild A/B overrides but are no longer required. WHY NO PER-SCENE AUTO-ADJUST IS NEEDED (user asked whether it must be manual / could auto-adapt to morning/evening/sunny/cloudy): the 709->P3 map is an exact, scene-independent colour-space conversion, and the red compression is PROPORTIONAL to each pixel's OWN red-over-green excess, so ONE value auto-scales across lighting (a cool/cloudy frame has little red excess -> barely touched; a warm/evening frame has more -> compressed proportionally) because the lighting is already encoded in the pixels. True scene-detecting auto-white-balance would be both redundant (the OBS/preview/video white balance is already correct — the video of the same frame is never red) and blind to the real red source (deferredmediad renders the saved still DOWNSTREAM of mediaserverd, so its output is unmeasurable from our hook — we can only pre-compensate, not react). If a single value ever fails to hold across lighting, the next step is a brightness-driven auto-scale (darker scenes, where Smart HDR/Deep Fusion over-cook more, compress more). Still-only, fail-open, preview/record byte-for-byte untouched; VCAM_PHOTO_REDKEEP still hot-overridable via /var/tmp/vcam_photored. 0.6.73: LUMINANCE-RAMPED red compression - fixes the two-subject conflict a single global red knob cannot. Device tuning showed it directly: at redkeep=60 skin went yellow/bloodless; raised to 85 the skin looked right but a clear/transparent glass cup glared PINK, because the same red the skin needs also tints the glass highlights. Root: skin (midtone) should stay warm but neutral specular highlights should stay neutral, and what separates them is LUMINANCE. So the red-EXCESS-over-green compression is now ramped by 709 luma (ramp 170->235): MIDTONE/skin pixels keep VCAM_PHOTO_REDKEEP (default bumped 60->85, device-confirmed skin value) of their red = blood colour preserved, while BRIGHT specular highlights ramp down to a lower floor VCAM_PHOTO_REDKEEP_HI (default 35, hot via /var/tmp/vcam_photohl) so neutral objects' highlights de-warm back toward white instead of glaring pink. Chosen after simulating BOTH a luma-ramp de-warm and a mild global desaturation on the actual on-device photo and viewing them: the luma-ramp cleaned the glass highlights while keeping the hand ruddy; the global desaturation just muted everything (the 0.6.70 wash-out family). Two hot knobs now: /var/tmp/vcam_photored = midtone/skin red-keep, /var/tmp/vcam_photohl = highlight red-keep floor. Still-only, fail-open, preview/record byte-for-byte untouched. 0.6.74: make the highlight ramp SELF-CALIBRATING (relative to the image's own white point) - fixes why 0.6.73 did nothing on device. 0.6.73 gated the ramp on an ABSOLUTE 709 luma (170->235) measured from the SAVED photo, but the compression runs on the pre-deferredmediad buffer which deferredmediad then BRIGHTENS, so the buffer's highlights never reached 170 and the ramp never engaged (device A/B: 85-flat vs 85/35 gave identical highlight R-G +53/+54, the glass stayed pink). This is itself a generalisation lesson: an absolute threshold did not even hold on the SAME scene. 0.6.74 replaces it with a two-pass loop - pass 1 builds a luma histogram of the buffer and takes its 98th-percentile as the image's own white point Lw; pass 2 ramps from VCAM_PHOTO_HITHRESH% of Lw up to 0.94*Lw. Because the window is a fraction of each image's own brightness, it engages regardless of deferredmediad's later brightening AND generalises across scenes/lighting. New hot knob /var/tmp/vcam_photolt (VCAM_PHOTO_HITHRESH, default 60) sets where the highlight ramp starts (lower = de-warm more of the brights; raise if skin highlights lose red). Trace log now prints lw= and lo=. Still-only, fail-open, preview/record byte-for-byte untouched. 0.6.75: TONE compensation - the real remaining issue was never colour, it was that the saved photo looks "fake" (washed, over-bright, glary) next to the natural OBS source. Pulling the user's correct source reference and measuring proved the COLOUR is already right (85-flat still highlight R-G = +55 = the source exactly); the difference is deferredmediad's Smart HDR BRIGHTENING and FLATTENING the still (clip 7.5% vs source 0.09%, half the contrast), which also explains why only the photo changes and video/preview do not. Simulating grades on the real photo showed exposure-down + contrast-up (hue-preserving) restores the natural moody tone, while a saturation boost just re-pinked the glass. So 0.6.75 adds a still-only TONE pass on the P3 buffer: VCAM_PHOTO_EXPOSURE (gain, <100 darkens) + VCAM_PHOTO_CONTRAST (S-curve on LUMA, RGB scaled by L'/L so brightness/contrast change but never hue/saturation), both hot via /var/tmp/vcam_expo + /var/tmp/vcam_contrast, both default 100 (off) pending on-device tuning against the source. Also retires the 0.6.73/0.6.74 highlight de-warm (VCAM_PHOTO_REDKEEP_HI default 35->85 = off): it made clear glass colder than the source and bit lit skin, and the glare was a tone problem not a hue one. Net default now: mode-2 gamut + flat redkeep 85 (hue-correct, device-proven) + tone off. Trace prints expo=/con=. Still-only, fail-open, preview/record byte-for-byte untouched; note this pre-compensates an ADAPTIVE Apple HDR pass downstream of the tweak, so it can reduce but may not fully erase the fake look. 0.6.76: BAKE the device-accepted REAR tone as the compiled default + DEAD-CODE CLEANUP. (1) The 0.6.75 tone pass, tuned on-device to VCAM_PHOTO_EXPOSURE=72 (darken to counter deferredmediad's Smart-HDR over-brighten) + VCAM_PHOTO_CONTRAST=138 (restore the source's contrast), is now the compiled default (was 100/100 = off) so the natural, non-"fake" look ships without needing the /var/tmp flags; both stay hot-overridable via /var/tmp/vcam_expo + /var/tmp/vcam_contrast. Net shipping default: mode-2 exact 709->P3 gamut map + flat redkeep 85 + expo 72 + contrast 138 — all REAR-camera-tuned (the front camera has a different sensor/exposure/HDR curve and may need its own set, tracked separately). (2) Removed the 0.6.66 photo-colour DIAGNOSTIC scaffolding now that the fix is settled: the three still-only, read-only probes (VCamDescribeBuffer buffer-attr dump, VCamCentreSample centre-pixel read, VCamPhotoDiagOnce fresh-709-vs-P3 transfer probe — 112 lines) and their callsite in VCamOverwriteInPlace (the log-once still-DST-native / after-xfer / photo-diag block; the +1-retained OBS-src diag var renamed srcForDiag->stillSrc). The mode-2 fix path is BYTE-FOR-BYTE unchanged (same VCamStillGamut709toP3 call, same args and order, same fail-open) — only diagnostic logging is gone; video/preview/record still untouched. Also corrected the stale VCAM_PHOTO_REDKEEP doc comment that still described a MIDTONE/skin luma range and the removed VCAM_PHOTO_REDKEEP_HI luminance ramp (the red-keep has been a FLAT red-excess-over-green pull since 0.6.75). No functional change to the shipping photo or video behaviour; this is a behaviour-preserving cleanup + default bake. 0.6.77: REVERT the baked tone default back to OFF (VCAM_PHOTO_EXPOSURE/CONTRAST 72/138 -> 100/100). On-device the 0.6.76 baked 72/138 made the REAR saved photo TOO DARK, and the device log proved why: the last-accepted 0.6.75 rear photo actually ran at expo=100 con=100 (the /var/tmp hot flags had been cleared under load, so 0.6.75 fell back to its compiled 100/100 = tone off), so 72/138 was never genuinely accepted - it was a recording error baked in 0.6.76. The REAR photo looks right from the mode-2 709->P3 gamut map + flat red-keep 85 ALONE; the darkening tone pass is not wanted for the rear default. The tone pass CODE is unchanged and still hot-tunable via /var/tmp/vcam_expo + /var/tmp/vcam_contrast (kept for the front camera, which is tuned separately, and for any future scene that shows deferredmediad's brighten/flatten "fake" look). Also note the correct device flag directory: mediaserverd's sandbox /var/tmp resolves to /rootfs/private/var/tmp (where OpenVCam.log lives), NOT the SSH shell's /var/tmp (a jbroot AppGroup path) - hot flags must be written to /rootfs/private/var/tmp to be read. Net shipping default now: mode-2 gamut + flat redkeep 85 + tone OFF. Still-only, fail-open, preview/record byte-for-byte untouched.
